<!--
    RE-friction round 2, need `keyboard-callback-uses-undefined`, challenge
    6989ca5da15272fa37a80c43 (x86-64 ELF `lugosiii`): `kuna decompile ... 0x6500` by address rendered the Wayland `wl_keyboard_listener` key handler as

        void sub_6500(long a0)
        {
          ...
          int v7; // r8d
          int v8; // r9d

          if (v8 != 1 || !*(long *)(a0 + 0x68))
            return;
          v2 = xkb_state_key_get_one_sym(*(long *)(a0 + 0x68),v7 + 8);

    (two values declared and then read with nothing ever assigning them.)  The
    disassembly says where they come from: the callback's ABI signature is
    key(void *data, struct wl_keyboard *, uint32_t serial, uint32_t time,
    uint32_t key, uint32_t state), so `key` arrives in r8d and `state` in r9d.

    Root cause (P4, own-input prototype recovery), which is NOT a missing
    prototype.  ActionInputPrototype registers a trial for every input Varnode
    and marks it ACTIVE when it has descendants, i.e. when the body genuinely
    reads the register before writing it; rdi, r8d and r9d all reach
    ParamListStandard::fillinMap as active trials, and buildTrialMap even
    synthesizes unreferenced filler trials for the ignored rsi/rdx/rcx.
    forceInactiveChain (fspec.cc:1519, maxchain = 2) then counts the run of
    unused slots and, once it passes two, marks EVERY remaining trial inactive
    (the active ones included) so the live-in registers past the hole never
    become parameters.  The witness's hole is exactly three registers wide, one
    past the limit.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "own-input-prototype-recovery".  A run of unused
    ARGUMENT REGISTERS inside the FUNCTION'S OWN input recovery does not veto a
    later register the body reads before writing (kuna_inputparamgap.rs,
    always on).  Call sites are untouched: there a trial is
    scored from the caller's data flow, where a live argument register really is
    ambiguous and a long empty run really is evidence the recovery ran past the
    argument list.

    The fixture is that shape, hand assembled.  `on_key` reads r9d and r8d with
    rsi/rdx/rcx ignored (a THREE-register hole); `on_two` is the control, the
    same construct with a TWO-register hole (rsi/rdx ignored, rcx read), which
    the upstream rule already tolerates.

    Under the upstream limit on_key takes one parameter and reads r8d/r9d as
    undefined locals; here the full six-parameter ABI signature is recovered,
    filler parameters and all, and `on_two` is unchanged.
-->
